.TH CBMAP_INTERNAL_NODE 3 "15 Mar 2023" "CB" "Library Functions"

.SH NAME
cbmap_internal_node_new, cbmap_internal_node_destroy \- Manage internal nodes in a CBMap

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB #include <lib/cbmap_internal.h>

.IB struct cbmap_internal_node*
.IB cbmap_internal_node_new (void);

.IB void
.IB cbmap_internal_node_destroy (struct cbmap_internal_node* node);

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBcbmap_internal_node_new\fR and \fBcbmap_internal_node_destroy\fR functions are used to manage internal nodes in a CBMap. These functions are essential for the structure and maintenance of the CBMap data structure.

.SH cbmap_internal_node_new
The \fBcbmap_internal_node_new\fR function creates a new internal node for a CBMap. It allocates memory for the node and initializes its fields to default values.

The function returns a pointer to the newly created internal node, or \fBNULL\fR if memory allocation fails.

.SH cbmap_internal_node_destroy
The \fBcbmap_internal_node_destroy\fR function destroys an internal node in a CBMap. It frees the memory allocated for the node. Note that this function assumes that all child nodes have already been freed.

The function takes one parameter:
.TP
.B node
A pointer to the internal node to be destroyed.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBcbmap_internal_node_new\fR and \fBcbmap_internal_node_destroy\fR functions to manage internal nodes in a CBMap:

.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <lib/cbmap_internal.h>

int main() {
    struct cbmap_internal_node* node = cbmap_internal_node_new();

    if(node != NULL) {
        // Use the internal node

        cbmap_internal_node_destroy(node);
    } else {
        printf("Memory allocation failed\n");
    }

    return 0;
}
.nef